name w3-10-package-q-1
timeout 480
time 1991-01-19T00:00:00Z
wx fixture wx-2026-07-27T00Z.wxb

# CAMPAIGN W3, SORTIE 10 OF 10 - PACKAGE Q, 19 JANUARY 1991. THE FULL PACKAGE.
# CHAIN TERMINUS. Its predecessor is w3-01-ew-radar.fbm, and the one thing that arrives here from it is
# the forward early-warning set `karinod`: if sortie 01 killed it, the campaign overlay DELETES its unit
# block and this file's net loses its western member. The sector node `karisoc`, which no earlier sortie
# can reach, keeps the net alive - so the carry degrades the defence's REACH rather than abolishing it,
# which is what a centralised IADS losing one border radar actually is.
# Spec, anchor and sources: doc/campaigns/w3-desert-storm.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
# CONTROL: none - it is the capstone and it moves everything at once.
#
# THE ANCHOR: 78 aircraft (56 F-16, 14 F-15C, 6 F-4G, 2 EF-111) against Al-Tuwaitha and downtown
# Baghdad, thousands of SAM and AAA rounds, an SA-3 confirmed on at least one F-16, 100 mm AAA, ~55
# Iraqi fighters aloft, TWO F-16 SHOT DOWN and both pilots taken prisoner [T4].
#
# THE SCALE SUBSTITUTION AND ITS DIRECTION, stated as a ratio and not as a mood: 78 aircraft become 24
# (16 F-16 in four flights - 8 strike, 4 escort, 4 "SEAD" with AGM-88 - against 8 MiG-29 in two
# flights), i.e. 31 % of the package and 15 % of the defending fighters. That is a SMALLER package
# against a PROPORTIONALLY LARGER fighter defence, and it is chosen because 24 aircraft plus their
# stores is the largest actor list this tree has been run with. The catalogue's `f15c` and `ef111` rows
# EXIST and are deliberately not flown: `f15c` is `ACCEPTED` as a FLIGHT MODEL only and no promotion
# gate measures a weapon, and `ef111` is a mover with no radar-jamming half at all (gap C13). The
# 100 mm AAA is a ZSU-23-4 that cannot reach the ingress altitude. EVERY ONE OF THOSE MAKES THE
# DEFENCE WEAKER THAN THE ANCHOR'S except the fighter ratio, which makes it stronger.
#
# WHAT `C15` COSTS THIS FILE, AND WHAT REPLACES IT. There is no time-on-target, no deconfliction and no
# lead tasking (gap C15): a FlightBox package is N flights that happen to be in the same file. THE
# SUBSTITUTE IS AUTHORED GEOMETRY, and it is stated so that nobody reads it as coordination: the SEAD
# flight is spawned 20.5 km from the S-125 on its own lane so that its shot falls at t ~ 7 s; the
# strikers are spawned 90 km out so that their releases fall at t ~ 175-230 s; the escorts are spawned
# between them so that the merge falls after that. The package's timing is the author's arithmetic, not
# a briefed TOT, and the run cannot measure a mistimed package because it cannot express a timed one.
#
# THE ARENA is the campaign's, whole: Al-Tuwaitha 33.20 N 44.52 E [T4, approximate], the ring at
# 44.32-44.62 E, the forward EW set at 43.60 E. `--elev const`, 0 m datum, no terrain masking (C4).
# Scale: 1 deg of longitude = 93 145 m [DERIVED]. `wx fixture` is the committed GFS blob.
# NIGHT: 03:00 local, sun about -48 deg [DERIVED]. Package Q was in fact a MORNING strike; the night is
# the campaign's setting (17 January) and not this sortie's clock, and it is declared because W3's own
# spec puts all ten missions at night. It costs the visual channel and nothing else.
#
# WHICH DEFENSIVE LAYERS THIS FILE MEASURES: BOTH, AND THAT IS THE ONE PLACE THE CAMPAIGN BREAKS ITS
# OWN RULE - deliberately, and with the price named. O5's rule says two layers cannot be measured in one
# file, and sorties 01-09 obey it. The capstone does not, because a package against an integrated air
# defence is the one thing that cannot be split without becoming a different question. THE PRICE: the
# run ends at the FIRST flight-monitor K.O., so no number here is a campaign-length statistic, and every
# one of them is reported with what was still airborne at that instant.
#
# THE VICTORY CONDITION THE SPEC WROTE: >= 6 of 8 strikers release AND >= 14 of 16 recover. It is NOT
# expressed as objectives, because `objective` has no "n of m" and the campaign will not invent one;
# it is read out of the log as a count of `pilot ATTACK_RELEASE mode=ccrp` lines and a count of
# `UNIT_RESULT` rows that are not FAIL.
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION: DOES A 24-AIRCRAFT MISSION RUN DETERMINISTICALLY, AND DOES THE PACKAGE
# STILL WORK AT THAT SIZE? Both halves are the result.
#
# MEASURED (fingerprint 35eff34494823487), AND THE SPEC'S VICTORY CONDITION IS MET ON BOTH HALVES:
#   strikers reaching release  8 OF 8 (t = 173 .. 271). Required: >= 6
#   F-16 recovered             15 OF 16 - only `qaesc4` is lost. Required: >= 14
#   the SEAD                   three AGM-88 into `karisa3` at 0.0028, 0.0052 and 1.10 m. THE SITE DIED
#                              ON THE FIRST, at t = 60.2; the other two arrived at t = 61.0 and 65.7
#                              into a corpse. THAT IS `C15` WITH A PRICE ON IT: with no deconfliction
#                              and no lead tasking, three quarters of a four-ship's anti-radiation
#                              expenditure went to one already-dead battery
#   the surviving battery      `karisa2` fired all six V-750 at t = 37.6 .. 80.7 at bearings 182 / 178 /
#                              174 / 150 / 148 / 147 - i.e. SOUTH, at the SEAD flight, not at the eight
#                              strikers behind it. Zero arrivals. The Weasel absorbs the magazine again
#   the air battle             Blue 4 AIM-120 solutions, Red 4 R-27R. `qaesc4` killed at 2.79 m,
#                              `qamia2` at 5.92 m after surviving 7.75 m, `qamib2` at 11.74 m
#   ground killed              `karisa3` `karisa2` `kariflk` `karihq` `karidra` - 5 of 8. `karitwa`
#                              (`target_hard`) survived two Mk 84 at ~21 m, `karisoc` survived
#   the scale answer           24 aircraft + 8 ground + 34 stores = 66 actors; 308.5 s of sim in 11.7 s
#                              of wall clock, and the campaign fingerprint is one hash over 9 runs at
#                              --threads 1/2/4
#   RED KILLED ONE OF ITS OWN  `qamia1_r27r_25`, launched at t = 275.0 with a solution on a target at
#                              32.98 N 44.63 E, detonated 11.74 m from `qamib2` - a MiG-29 of the OTHER
#                              Red flight - at t = 293.2 and killed it. The proximity fuze is resolved
#                              by the OWNER against every published pose except the launcher's, with no
#                              team test, which is the same boundary that makes a seeker blind to
#                              identity. At 24 aircraft that becomes an attrition channel: 1 of the 3
#                              aircraft lost in this file was killed by its own side
# THE CARRY, MEASURED BOTH WAYS (this file run as campaign step 10 against the identical file run
# STANDALONE): `net CUE` 16 against 25 - the opening night's bomb costs the defence 9 of 25 cue
# messages. EVERYTHING ELSE IS THE SAME: same 8 releases, same 8 `site LAUNCH`, same first `site
# RADIATE` (t = 0.0), `site TRACK` (6.8), `net CUE` (8.0) and `site LAUNCH` (37.5), same three losses,
# same five ground kills, same 308.5 s run length. 30 of 58 telemetry files are byte-identical and the
# other 28 differ in 1 to 13 columns, ALL of them RWR or datalink bookkeeping - not one trajectory
# column moves. SO: KILLING A FORWARD EARLY-WARNING RADAR IS WORTH THE WHOLE STRIKE WHEN IT IS THE
# NET'S ONLY NODE (sortie 01 against 02) AND 36 % OF THE CUE TRAFFIC WHEN IT IS NOT (here). The value
# of that bomb is a property of the net's TOPOLOGY, not of the bomb.
#
# WHAT TO READ:
#   events.log      count `pilot ATTACK_RELEASE mode=ccrp` (strikers that reached release), count
#                   `UNIT_RESULT ... result=FAIL` among the sixteen F-16, then `site LAUNCH` /
#                   `net CUE` / first `site RADIATE` - the last three are what the carry moves
#   campaign-summary.txt  the step's own fingerprint, and `campaign ATTRITION` / `campaign EXPENDED`
#
# READING RULE: THE EXIT CODE IS NOT THE VERDICT. Read, in this order: (1) strikers that reached
# release, (2) F-16 lost, (3) `site LAUNCH` count and first `site RADIATE` time, (4) the wall-clock and
# the fingerprint, because the scale half of the question is answered by those two and by nothing else.
# State what was still in the air when the run ended beside every one of them.

net kari
  control karisoc
  link wire
  period 4.0
  hold 3
  wcs free
  member karisoc
  member karinod
  member karisa2 autonomy hold
  member karisa3 autonomy free
  member kariflk autonomy hold

unit qawsl1
  module f16
  team friendly
  flight wa 1
  spawn 33.06000 44.22000 5000 90.0 480
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set fcr_mode off
  set attack_mode arm
  set arm_class fire_control
  set rwr on
  set datalink on
  set store 3 agm88
  set store 7 agm88
  set cmds_mode auto
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 60
  set cmds_flare 60
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task attack
  wp 33.06000 44.30000 5000 480
  wp 33.06000 43.70000 5000 480
